 [:td {:colspan "2"} "Total of best lifts in military press, snatch, and clean &amp; jerk determined placement."]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "This class saw one of the most dominant performances in Olympic weightlifting history, as Egyptian " [:a {:href "/athletes/55533"} "Khadr El-Touni"] " won by 35 kg, bettering his own world record with a total of 387.5 kg. That mark would have won him the " [:a {:href "/results/28870"} "light-heavyweight division"] " by 15 kg. El-Touni had the highest lift for all three phases of the competition, setting world records in both the press and snatch. El-Touni went on to win three World titles (1946, and 1949-50), placing 3rd at the 1951 World Championships. He also competed in the " [:a {:href "/results/28940"} "1948 Olympics"] ", but illness prevented him from successfully defending his title, and he placed fourth. El-Touni also recorded 16 world records, although only 11 of them were recognized."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Well back in Berlin were the silver and bronze medalists, both Germans: " [:a {:href "/athletes/55832"} "Rudolf Ismayr"] ", the defending gold medalist, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/55871"} "Adolf Wagner"] ". Wagner would win silver (1937) and gold (1938) in this class at the next two World Championships, while Ismayr would win silver behind Wagner in 1938."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Kilograms"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/55533"} "Khadr El-Touni"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/EGY"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/EGY.png"}] "EGY"]] [:td {} "387.5"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "label label-default"} [:abbrev {:title "Olympic Record"} "OR"]]] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:h